WEBVTT

00:01.000 --> 00:04.805
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.905 --> 00:08.173
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.332 --> 00:11.885
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.919 --> 00:14.885
♪ On which we used to rely?

00:14.919 --> 00:18.389
♪ Lucky there's a family guy

00:18.423 --> 00:21.676
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.710 --> 00:23.243
♪ All the things that make us

00:23.277 --> 00:24.629
♪ Laugh and cry

00:24.663 --> 00:26.856
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:35.953 --> 00:37.619
All right, class,
today we're going

00:37.653 --> 00:39.971
to be talking about variables.

00:40.006 --> 00:41.639
(kids laughing)

00:41.673 --> 00:43.707
Okay, it's just a rear end.

00:43.725 --> 00:45.090
We all have one.

00:45.141 --> 00:47.742
Mine just happens to be
a hot Brazilian woman's.

00:47.776 --> 00:49.228
No, we're not laughing at you.

00:49.262 --> 00:51.779
We're laughing at
Griffin's shoes.

00:51.813 --> 00:53.446
Why, what's wrong with them?

00:53.481 --> 00:54.632
I don't even know
what those are.

00:54.666 --> 00:55.864
What's "Teslik"?

00:55.899 --> 00:58.267
Um, I'm not sure,
but my mom says they're

00:58.301 --> 01:00.468
very popular
with Latvian athletes.

01:01.771 --> 01:05.139
Running, throwing,
lifting big things.

01:06.574 --> 01:07.807
Teslik.

01:07.841 --> 01:10.210
That's all right with me, man.

01:10.245 --> 01:12.245
<i>ANNOUNCER:
Each shoe sold separate.</i>

01:12.279 --> 01:13.912
Are they really that bad?

01:13.946 --> 01:16.080
(laughs): Are you kidding?
They're terrible.

01:16.114 --> 01:18.046
Man, high school is rough.

01:18.081 --> 01:19.332
This year it's sneakers.

01:19.350 --> 01:21.317
Last year it was all about
being overweight

01:21.352 --> 01:22.769
and wearing a baseball cap.

01:24.269 --> 01:25.852
Hey, guys,
sure hope we win

01:25.886 --> 01:27.573
the big game
on Saturday.

01:27.607 --> 01:28.605
Get lost.
Dork.

01:28.639 --> 01:30.455
Yeah, gain some
weight, will ya?

01:30.489 --> 01:31.941
(laughing)

01:31.975 --> 01:35.262
Oh, of all the years
to be trim and well-hatted.

01:38.313 --> 01:41.014
That was a fun birthday
party, huh, Stewie?

01:41.049 --> 01:43.532
That kid was Indian
and 11 years old.

01:43.567 --> 01:45.068
Where are you
finding my friends?

01:45.103 --> 01:47.236
Oh, someone's a
little cranky.

01:47.271 --> 01:48.969
Here, let me put
on the radio.

01:49.004 --> 01:50.272
♪ Talkin' 'bout the club

01:50.306 --> 01:51.805
♪ Lookin' round
the club ♪

01:51.840 --> 01:53.189
♪ Pimpin' at the club

01:53.223 --> 01:54.857
♪ Emphasizing "club."

01:54.892 --> 01:56.308
Yuck, this
is awful.

01:56.342 --> 02:01.077
ANNE MURRAY: ♪ Or am I really
lying here with you? ♪

02:01.128 --> 02:04.030
My God, who is this enchantress?

02:04.048 --> 02:06.398
♪ Baby

02:06.433 --> 02:08.150
♪ You take me in your arms
Oh, isn't that cute?

02:08.182 --> 02:10.369
My little Stewie
likes Anne Murray.

02:10.387 --> 02:12.221
She sounds like an angel.

02:12.255 --> 02:16.255
It's like her voice is putting
my entire body in her mouth.

02:16.306 --> 02:17.891
♪

02:17.925 --> 02:21.159
♪ And, oh, I

02:21.193 --> 02:24.996
♪ Just fall in love again

02:25.031 --> 02:31.400
♪ Just one touch and then
it happens every time ♪

02:31.434 --> 02:35.553
♪ And there I go, I

02:35.571 --> 02:38.454
♪ Just fall in love again

02:38.488 --> 02:41.356
♪ And when I do

02:42.392 --> 02:45.076
♪ I can't help myself

02:45.110 --> 02:48.080
♪ I fall in love with you.

02:48.114 --> 02:50.081
(music fades)

02:50.115 --> 02:51.714
You like that, Stewie?

02:51.748 --> 02:53.233
You like
Anne Murray's music?

02:53.251 --> 02:54.500
Do I?

02:54.534 --> 02:55.685
I've never known
such happiness.

02:55.720 --> 02:57.369
This must be what
it's like for you

02:57.403 --> 02:59.837
when you're buying potatoes
or scraping your heels

02:59.871 --> 03:02.706
or smushing on makeup
for a sad night out.

03:06.976 --> 03:09.055
<i>ANNOUNCER: This portion
of the Channel 5 News at 6:00</i>

03:09.077 --> 03:11.276
<i>is brought to you buy condoms.</i>

03:11.311 --> 03:12.679
<i>Put us on backwards
a little bit,</i>

03:12.713 --> 03:14.714
<i>then put us on the right way.</i>

03:14.749 --> 03:17.565
Mom, Dad, I need to
get new sneakers.

03:17.599 --> 03:19.834
What? I just bought you
new sneakers.

03:19.868 --> 03:21.735
I know, but I
need cooler ones.

03:21.769 --> 03:23.120
Shut up, and stop complaining.

03:23.154 --> 03:24.738
When I was your age,
I didn't even have sneakers.

03:24.772 --> 03:26.539
We wore stale hamburger buns.

03:26.573 --> 03:27.724
No, you didn't, Peter.

03:27.759 --> 03:28.890
Shh, he doesn't know that.

03:28.924 --> 03:30.058
He's just a dumb, fat loser.

03:30.092 --> 03:31.392
Did you see his shoes?

03:31.426 --> 03:33.961
Look, Chris, I'm sorry,
but money's tight right now.

03:33.995 --> 03:35.713
Yeah, I even had
to take a second job

03:35.747 --> 03:37.680
as a door-to-door
vacuum salesman.

03:37.715 --> 03:38.682
This thing can pick up anything.

03:38.717 --> 03:39.949
Here's a little demonstration.

03:39.983 --> 03:41.982
I'll pour some wine,
rub in some feces,

03:42.017 --> 03:44.535
and, to top it off,
some mustard mixed with feces.

03:44.569 --> 03:45.853
Tough stain, right?

03:45.887 --> 03:47.587
Oh, crap, I forgot the vacuum.

03:47.621 --> 03:49.621
(crickets chirping)

03:50.958 --> 03:52.273
(Anne Murray singing "Snowbird")

03:52.291 --> 03:55.877
All right, Rupert, you ready to
get lost in some Anne?

03:55.911 --> 03:59.712
♪ Gentle breezes where
the peaceful waters flow ♪

03:59.746 --> 04:03.934
♪ Spread your tiny wings
and fly away ♪

04:03.968 --> 04:05.784
♪ And take the snow
back with you ♪

04:05.802 --> 04:10.270
♪ Where it came from
on that day ♪

04:10.305 --> 04:14.622
♪ The one I love forever
is untrue ♪

04:14.640 --> 04:19.976
♪ And if I could, you know
that I would fly away with you ♪

04:20.010 --> 04:25.264
♪ Yeah, if I could, you know
that I would fly-i-i-i-i ♪

04:25.298 --> 04:30.067
♪ Away with you.

04:30.101 --> 04:32.101
(song ends)

04:33.805 --> 04:35.404
What a woman.

04:35.439 --> 04:37.038
To think Canada's got her,

04:37.073 --> 04:40.308
and we have Kelly Clarkson going
to town at a Waffle House.

04:50.116 --> 04:52.216
That's it,
I'm getting those sneakers.

04:52.250 --> 04:54.651
After all, shoes make the man.

04:54.685 --> 04:56.187
Gentlemen, take your marks.

04:56.221 --> 04:57.820
(pistol fires)

04:57.854 --> 04:59.089
(crowd cheering)

04:59.123 --> 05:01.841
I'm a different kind of winner.

05:04.591 --> 05:07.226
Oh, come on, Dad,
there's no cash in here.

05:07.260 --> 05:10.595
Just a bunch of cards that say,
"I farted on your car,"

05:10.629 --> 05:12.563
with his insurance info.

05:15.951 --> 05:17.968
(moans sadly)

05:19.488 --> 05:20.854
Busted.

05:20.889 --> 05:22.454
Geez, it's so weird
that Mom and Dad

05:22.488 --> 05:24.989
would leave the house
without their purse and wallet.

05:25.023 --> 05:26.625
I wonder where they are?

05:26.659 --> 05:28.025
Can I get you anything else?

05:28.059 --> 05:30.293
Uh, two more
Cokes, please.

05:30.327 --> 05:32.644
Could you excuse me?

05:32.679 --> 05:34.979
(keypad beeping)

05:35.013 --> 05:37.182
(phone rings)

05:37.200 --> 05:39.333
Hello.

05:39.368 --> 05:40.684
Damone, it's Peter.

05:40.718 --> 05:42.484
I'm at my date and I left
my wallet at home.

05:42.518 --> 05:44.086
Will you please borrow
your mom's car,

05:44.121 --> 05:45.400
drive to my house,
get my wallet,

05:45.421 --> 05:46.622
and bring it back here?

05:48.706 --> 05:51.225
Damone, are you there?
Damone?

05:53.828 --> 05:56.379
Yeesh, I'm really kind of busy.

05:56.413 --> 05:58.196
Come on,
just do me this one favor.

05:58.214 --> 06:00.631
All right,
but you owe me for this one.

06:00.665 --> 06:02.284
Oh, thanks, Damone.

06:02.318 --> 06:04.820
And that was the last
we ever saw of him.

06:10.679 --> 06:11.847
Well, well, well.

06:11.881 --> 06:13.913
Who do we have here?

06:13.948 --> 06:15.982
It's me, Chris,
you know me.

06:16.017 --> 06:17.716
Why are you talking
like a bad guy?

06:17.751 --> 06:20.852
Listen, I saw you steal
money from Mom's purse.

06:20.886 --> 06:22.586
Oh...

06:22.620 --> 06:24.088
Well, I was just trying
to get new sneakers.

06:24.123 --> 06:26.555
You're not going to
tell on me, are you?

06:26.590 --> 06:29.759
Well, that depends.
On what?

06:29.794 --> 06:32.061
How about, I won't tell
Mom and Dad about the money

06:32.095 --> 06:35.130
if you promise to
do whatever I say.

06:35.163 --> 06:36.931
Meg, this is how a
lot of porn starts.

06:36.966 --> 06:37.964
I mean it, Chris.

06:37.998 --> 06:39.032
I'll tell them.

06:39.066 --> 06:40.682
Mom, Chris took
money out of the...

06:40.717 --> 06:41.834
Okay, okay, okay.

06:41.869 --> 06:44.936
I'll do whatever you want.

06:44.971 --> 06:46.404
I thought you'd
see it that way.

06:46.438 --> 06:48.673
Here's a list of stuff
I need you to do for me.

06:48.707 --> 06:51.490
Shouldn't have taken
that money, Chris.

06:51.524 --> 06:53.392
Eh, she's right.
I'm a horrible person.

06:53.426 --> 06:55.111
I'm only one
step above those people

06:55.146 --> 06:58.812
who really like dive bars and
really need you to know it.

06:58.846 --> 07:00.346
I love this place.
I love dive bars.

07:00.380 --> 07:01.581
Dive bars are my favorite.

07:01.616 --> 07:03.248
They're so much better
than regular bars

07:03.282 --> 07:04.316
because worse is better.

07:04.350 --> 07:05.350
I know, right?

07:05.383 --> 07:06.401
They're my favorite, too.

07:06.435 --> 07:07.436
I'm quirky like that.

07:07.471 --> 07:08.453
I've got quirky taste.

07:08.487 --> 07:09.704
I'm a quirky girl.

07:09.739 --> 07:11.789
It seems like we would be
perfect for each other.

07:11.823 --> 07:14.625
But really we're
terrible for everyone.

07:20.812 --> 07:22.613
ANNE MURRAY:
♪ And though I...

07:22.648 --> 07:24.447
What the hell?

07:24.482 --> 07:26.065
Are you listening
to Anne Murray?

07:26.099 --> 07:28.866
Uh, I am experiencing
Anne Murray.

07:28.901 --> 07:30.767
Why? That music
is complete crap.

07:30.802 --> 07:33.136
Um, excuse me?

07:33.171 --> 07:35.055
It's all just vapid,
overproduced tripe.

07:35.089 --> 07:36.389
It has no edge whatsoever.

07:36.423 --> 07:38.423
Yeah, neither does a
soft summer breeze,

07:38.457 --> 07:39.859
or a letter from
an old friend.

07:39.893 --> 07:41.492
I'm pretty sure
envelopes have edges.

07:41.526 --> 07:43.526
Fine, an e-mail. Dick.

07:43.560 --> 07:45.130
Whatever. You know what?
I don't care.

07:45.164 --> 07:47.348
I think Anne Murray sucks,
and that's my opinion.

07:47.399 --> 07:48.681
Oh, yeah?
Well, I'm going to make

07:48.716 --> 07:50.049
a believer out of you, Brian.

07:50.085 --> 07:52.285
I am going to show you the true
meaning of Anne Murray.

07:52.319 --> 07:54.478
Don't waste your time,
I'm not going to change my mind.

07:54.486 --> 07:55.919
I don't know, Bri, I can
make things happen

07:55.953 --> 07:57.188
when I want to.

07:57.222 --> 07:58.855
Just ask my twin brother.

07:58.906 --> 08:01.373
(grunting)
And here's baby boy number one.

08:01.408 --> 08:04.976
I understand you've decided
to name him Stewie.

08:04.994 --> 08:06.460
One more to go.

08:06.494 --> 08:08.394
And here he... oh.

08:08.428 --> 08:09.596
Oh, dear.

08:09.630 --> 08:11.997
Oh, no,
did Dave not make it?

08:15.602 --> 08:17.135
Okay, Meg,
I've cleaned your room

08:17.169 --> 08:18.384
and I did your homework.

08:18.419 --> 08:19.585
Can I be done now?

08:19.620 --> 08:21.872
Oh, no, there's a
lot more things

08:21.906 --> 08:23.408
I need you to do for me.

08:23.440 --> 08:24.574
There are?
Like what?

08:24.608 --> 08:25.940
Well, now you
have to watch

08:25.974 --> 08:27.476
<i>An Officer and
a Gentlemen with me.</i>

08:27.510 --> 08:28.609
And that's it?

08:28.643 --> 08:30.278
(laughs)
No.

08:30.313 --> 08:31.778
At the end,
when Richard Gere

08:31.812 --> 08:33.612
carries Debra Winger
out of the paper mill,

08:33.646 --> 08:37.516
you have to read what's
on this index card.

08:37.550 --> 08:41.318
(on TV): ♪ Love lift us up
where we belong... ♪

08:41.352 --> 08:42.720
(crying)

08:42.755 --> 08:44.553
That's going to be
you some day, Meg.

08:44.588 --> 08:45.756
I know it will.

08:45.790 --> 08:49.023
I just know it will.

08:51.325 --> 08:53.027
Okay, Chris, I want
to wear contacts,

08:53.062 --> 08:54.394
but I'm too squeamish
to put them in myself,

08:54.428 --> 08:56.561
so you need to help me.

08:56.596 --> 08:57.997
Really?
Is it that hard?

08:58.032 --> 08:59.399
My eyes are
too sensitive.

08:59.433 --> 09:00.600
It's like they
overreact or something.

09:00.634 --> 09:02.183
Okay.

09:05.203 --> 09:06.469
Ah, what happened
to your eye?!

09:06.504 --> 09:08.406
Don't worry about it.
Just put the lens in.

09:08.440 --> 09:09.905
But where do I put it?

09:09.939 --> 09:10.974
The pupil's gone.

09:11.008 --> 09:12.608
Your eye just looks
like a ping-pong ball.

09:12.642 --> 09:14.291
I think it rolled
up into my head.

09:14.309 --> 09:16.210
Look, just... just
do the other one.

09:17.145 --> 09:19.095
(screaming)

09:19.129 --> 09:21.629
You look like one of
those blind jazz guys.

09:21.664 --> 09:23.699
Where are you?
I can't see anything.

09:23.734 --> 09:26.301
(screaming)

09:26.319 --> 09:27.384
I don't like this.

09:27.419 --> 09:28.752
Just wear your
stupid glasses.

09:28.787 --> 09:29.802
Chris, I'm blind.

09:29.820 --> 09:31.636
I'm sorry, Meg.

09:31.654 --> 09:33.022
(crying)

09:39.995 --> 09:41.845
What the hell
is all this?

09:41.879 --> 09:43.279
This is history, Brian.

09:43.314 --> 09:44.747
A key moment in the
history of happiness.

09:44.781 --> 09:45.982
Have a seat.

09:46.016 --> 09:48.183
(sighs)

09:50.035 --> 09:52.669
(piano plays gently)

09:52.703 --> 09:55.571
♪ I cried a tear

09:55.605 --> 09:59.373
♪ You wiped it dry

09:59.424 --> 10:02.926
♪ I was confused

10:02.961 --> 10:07.629
♪ You cleared my mind

10:07.663 --> 10:11.199
♪ I sold my soul

10:11.234 --> 10:14.350
♪ You bought it back for me

10:14.385 --> 10:17.554
♪ And held me up

10:17.588 --> 10:22.306
♪ And gave me dignity

10:22.341 --> 10:24.941
♪ Somehow you needed me

10:24.976 --> 10:28.895
♪ You gave me strength

10:28.946 --> 10:32.614
♪ To stand alone again

10:32.648 --> 10:36.484
♪ To face the world

10:36.518 --> 10:40.035
♪ Out on my own again

10:40.070 --> 10:43.705
♪ You put me high

10:43.739 --> 10:49.360
♪ Upon a pedestal

10:49.378 --> 10:55.981
♪ So high that I could almost
see eternity ♪

10:56.015 --> 10:59.866
♪ You needed me

10:59.884 --> 11:06.555
♪ You needed me...

11:06.590 --> 11:08.590
(song ends)

11:11.560 --> 11:12.875
(sniffles)

11:12.893 --> 11:14.743
Some hardcore
(bleep), right?

11:14.777 --> 11:16.612
Wow, thank you
for turning me on

11:16.647 --> 11:18.213
to something
so amazing.

11:18.247 --> 11:19.914
Hey, I'm just glad
you enjoyed it.

11:19.948 --> 11:21.215
And I'm sure
Buzz Lightyear

11:21.249 --> 11:24.185
would have enjoyed it if he
had bothered to show up.

11:25.886 --> 11:27.719
Hey, how would you like
to go to my apartment...

11:27.754 --> 11:29.987
and beyond?

11:33.559 --> 11:35.559
Okay, Meg,
I stood outside

11:35.593 --> 11:37.560
Planned Parenthood
for 36 hours

11:37.594 --> 11:38.628
and here are
the pictures

11:38.663 --> 11:40.411
of every girl in
your class who went in.

11:40.446 --> 11:41.530
All right, good.

11:41.564 --> 11:42.796
Now I want you
to call them

11:42.831 --> 11:44.998
as if you're
their dead baby.

11:45.033 --> 11:47.449
No way, that was the last
thing on your list.

11:47.483 --> 11:49.235
I-I can't do this anymore.

11:49.253 --> 11:50.385
Well, you should've
thought of that

11:50.419 --> 11:52.255
before you stole money out
of Mom's purse.

11:52.289 --> 11:53.838
I own you.

11:53.872 --> 11:55.606
Now, here's my
post office key.

11:55.640 --> 11:56.974
I need you to
go downtown

11:57.008 --> 11:58.174
and get the stuff
from my P.O. box.

11:58.209 --> 11:59.876
You-you don't get
your mail here?

11:59.910 --> 12:01.911
No, I get a lot of
private correspondence

12:01.929 --> 12:03.110
from the Netherlands.

12:03.145 --> 12:04.147
Like what?

12:04.181 --> 12:05.479
Like, I'm part
of a group that

12:05.514 --> 12:07.914
kind of trashes Anne
Frank's house every year.

12:07.949 --> 12:09.584
You know what?
No. That's it.

12:09.618 --> 12:12.684
I'm not-I'm not doing
any more of your crap, Meg.

12:12.718 --> 12:14.236
Chris, don't you see?
You have no choice.

12:14.271 --> 12:16.839
I'll just go tell Mom
and Dad about the money

12:16.873 --> 12:19.474
and they'll ground
your ass for a year.

12:19.508 --> 12:20.924
I don't care, tell them.

12:20.942 --> 12:22.410
'Cause I'm not even
going to be here.

12:22.444 --> 12:25.376
I'm going to run away and
no one will ever see me again.

12:25.410 --> 12:26.845
Good, go.

12:26.879 --> 12:28.028
And who's even
going to miss you?

12:28.063 --> 12:29.314
You're just a
little thief.

12:29.365 --> 12:31.064
Where you going, Chris?

12:31.098 --> 12:32.666
Who are you going to turn to?

12:32.701 --> 12:35.085
You don't even have any friends.

12:35.120 --> 12:36.752
(doorbell rings)

12:36.786 --> 12:38.819
Hi. I ran away from home.

12:38.854 --> 12:41.690
Do you mind if I live with you?

12:41.724 --> 12:43.089
Live with me?

12:43.124 --> 12:44.492
My goodness, I feel like

12:44.526 --> 12:47.527
I want to pinch myself
to see if I'm dreaming.

12:49.362 --> 12:52.230
Hot dog, it's real.

12:58.992 --> 13:02.227
Mr. Herbert, it's 6:00,
I'm-I'm not really tired yet.

13:02.261 --> 13:04.261
Well, you had those
three cups of Nyquil.

13:04.296 --> 13:05.995
You'll be down
soon enough.

13:06.030 --> 13:08.130
Okay, good night,
Mr. Herbert.

13:08.164 --> 13:10.631
Sweet dreams, Chris.

13:14.703 --> 13:18.372
Mr. Herbert, what's
life really all about?

13:18.406 --> 13:21.474
Well, I suppose it's
about trying new things;

13:21.508 --> 13:23.675
sampling the sweet
and the savory;

13:23.709 --> 13:25.910
not being afraid
to take everything

13:25.945 --> 13:28.812
life wants to
throw up on into ya.

13:28.847 --> 13:29.913
Wow.

13:29.948 --> 13:31.314
I never looked at it
like that before.

13:31.348 --> 13:32.649
Thanks, Mr. Herbert.

13:36.953 --> 13:38.519
But don't you
think it's scary

13:38.553 --> 13:39.787
to try new things?

13:39.821 --> 13:41.621
No, Chris.

13:41.655 --> 13:43.157
Life is like a
new baseball glove.

13:43.191 --> 13:44.858
At first you think

13:44.892 --> 13:46.624
you're never going to get
a ball in there.

13:46.658 --> 13:48.926
But then you oil it up,
work your fingers

13:48.961 --> 13:51.395
around in there a little,
and pretty soon

13:51.429 --> 13:53.561
you're pitching and catching.

13:53.596 --> 13:55.767
I guess that
makes sense.

13:55.801 --> 13:58.500
(farting)

13:58.534 --> 14:00.403
CHRIS:
Sorry.

14:00.437 --> 14:02.337
You really know how
to waste a Cialis,

14:02.371 --> 14:04.338
don't you?

14:06.940 --> 14:08.473
ANNE MURRAY:
♪ The snowbird sings the song

14:08.508 --> 14:11.342
♪ He always sings...

14:11.376 --> 14:12.944
You know,
this is definitely

14:12.979 --> 14:14.178
a mystery song for me.

14:14.212 --> 14:16.112
It's so upbeat,
but the lyrics are so dark.

14:16.147 --> 14:17.881
I'm-I'm sorry, are we both
listening to "Snowbird,"

14:17.915 --> 14:19.715
as recorded by
Miss Anne Murray?

14:19.749 --> 14:20.850
Yeah, the woman
in the song

14:20.885 --> 14:22.284
is trying to cope
with getting older

14:22.302 --> 14:23.469
and she feels
trapped because

14:23.503 --> 14:25.403
she knows she can't
do anything about it.

14:25.454 --> 14:26.820
No, no, no, no.

14:26.854 --> 14:28.489
I mean, you're right that
she's feeling trapped,

14:28.524 --> 14:29.755
but the message
is positive.

14:29.790 --> 14:31.255
It's about being trapped
in a bad relationship

14:31.289 --> 14:33.527
and having the guts
to get out of it.

14:33.561 --> 14:35.094
No, no. See, what I hear,
is that the woman

14:35.128 --> 14:36.410
is starting to hate life.

14:36.444 --> 14:39.278
You know the line, "Now I feel
such emptiness within"?

14:39.314 --> 14:40.915
Yeah, I know the song.

14:40.966 --> 14:42.231
Well, Anne Murray feels old.

14:42.266 --> 14:43.299
And then, to make things worse,

14:43.333 --> 14:44.866
the snowbird comes
around every year,

14:44.901 --> 14:46.717
bringing the snow, or "death,"

14:46.751 --> 14:48.170
to remind her that no matter
what she does,

14:48.204 --> 14:50.438
her fate's already written.

14:50.472 --> 14:53.089
Brian, the snowbird has nothing
to do with time or fate.

14:53.124 --> 14:54.356
Well, I disagree.

14:54.390 --> 14:55.691
Look, there's only one
way to settle this.

14:55.725 --> 14:58.427
Only the artist knows the
true meaning of their art.

14:59.962 --> 15:01.427
Now, Ms. O'Keefe, the flowers
in your paintings,

15:01.461 --> 15:03.530
what do they represent?

15:03.564 --> 15:06.764
Oh, wow,
that's a really good question.

15:06.799 --> 15:08.367
(sucks air)

15:08.401 --> 15:10.268
La, la, la...

15:10.302 --> 15:11.337
(blathering)

15:11.371 --> 15:13.838
Mm... (blathering)

15:13.873 --> 15:16.340
(blowing raspberries)

15:19.942 --> 15:21.042
Oh, hi, Meg.

15:21.077 --> 15:23.378
Could you tell Chris
breakfast is ready?

15:23.412 --> 15:26.080
Oh, um, I-I-I think
he got up early.

15:26.114 --> 15:28.715
He, um, said something
about a fat kid rally

15:28.749 --> 15:30.048
at Little Caesars.

15:30.083 --> 15:31.949
Huh, it's not like Chris
to miss breakfast.

15:31.984 --> 15:33.317
Oh, don't worry, Mom.

15:33.352 --> 15:35.919
I... I'm sure
he'll be home later.

15:35.953 --> 15:37.052
Well, I hope so.

15:37.087 --> 15:38.805
We were supposed
to go to the library

15:38.840 --> 15:40.038
to wipe boogers
in fancy books.

15:40.072 --> 15:41.705
I like to wipe mine
in Bronte novels.

15:41.739 --> 15:45.259
It's like a time bomb to
gross out lonely chicks.

15:45.293 --> 15:47.327
Oh, Heathcliff.

15:47.362 --> 15:48.545
Oh, come on.

15:48.581 --> 15:51.081
I want so little.

15:54.151 --> 15:56.519
Chris, what did you
do with my video tapes?

15:56.553 --> 15:59.284
I had a whole system.

15:59.319 --> 16:00.854
I was trying to find a
good movie to watch.

16:00.888 --> 16:02.454
I've never even heard of
most of these.

16:02.489 --> 16:04.057
"Jeffrey at the Park."

16:04.091 --> 16:06.575
"Mikey's Scoliosis Exam."

16:06.609 --> 16:10.126
"Nephew Somersault Compilation."

16:10.161 --> 16:11.641
Well, even if you
never heard of them,

16:11.662 --> 16:12.928
I assume you heard
of the alphabet.

16:12.963 --> 16:15.631
You want to explain to me how
"Sammy Popsicle"

16:15.665 --> 16:18.800
comes before "Napping, Various"?

16:18.834 --> 16:20.016
Geez, I'm sorry.

16:20.051 --> 16:21.719
Well, can we
watch "Lost Boys"?

16:21.754 --> 16:22.953
I've heard of that one.

16:22.987 --> 16:26.356
Those... those are
different lost boys.

16:30.909 --> 16:33.494
(whistling "Snowbird")

16:33.545 --> 16:34.665
Are you whistling
"Snowbird"?

16:34.712 --> 16:35.945
Yeah.

16:35.963 --> 16:36.996
That's awesome.

16:37.045 --> 16:39.532
Whoa, whoa, whoa,
why do you get to ring it?

16:39.565 --> 16:40.866
Because I was
into her first.

16:40.900 --> 16:42.782
(doorbell rings)
Let's flip for it, or...

16:42.900 --> 16:44.068
I can't believe
you did that.

16:44.102 --> 16:45.635
I cannot believe
you just did that.

16:45.670 --> 16:46.821
(doorbell rings)
Will you stop it.

16:46.855 --> 16:48.003
She's going to
think we're weird.

16:48.038 --> 16:49.054
Hi. Can I help you?

16:49.089 --> 16:50.088
Hi. Miss Murray.

16:50.122 --> 16:51.523
My name's Brian Griffin.

16:51.557 --> 16:53.324
Uh, first of all,
I just want to tell you

16:53.359 --> 16:54.890
what a huge fan I am.

16:54.925 --> 16:56.460
Aw, that's very
sweet of you.

16:56.494 --> 16:57.626
And I'm Stewie.

16:57.660 --> 17:00.496
I'm the one who sent
you the vibrators.

17:00.530 --> 17:01.998
Well, I'm glad you
enjoy my music.

17:02.032 --> 17:03.164
Would you like to come in?

17:03.199 --> 17:04.417
We'd love to.

17:04.451 --> 17:05.950
Wow, nothing says success

17:06.002 --> 17:08.384
like free on a Tuesday
at 3:00 p.m.

17:08.419 --> 17:10.453
I'll get there.

17:11.588 --> 17:13.055
So, Miss Murray,
the reason we came

17:13.089 --> 17:14.854
is that we have a question
about "Snowbird."

17:14.889 --> 17:16.474
It's about
a relationship, right?

17:16.508 --> 17:18.476
No, no, it's obviously
about the fear of growing old.

17:18.510 --> 17:20.228
You know, I think
you're both kind of right.

17:20.243 --> 17:21.911
I guess I always thought it was
about human limitation,

17:21.946 --> 17:24.463
both mental and physical.

17:24.497 --> 17:27.798
Okay, that...
that just blew my face off.

17:27.832 --> 17:29.132
Well, I hope
that helps.

17:29.167 --> 17:31.051
It does. Thank you so much,
Miss Murray.

17:31.085 --> 17:32.319
This has been terrific.

17:32.353 --> 17:33.485
Hey, uh, before we go,

17:33.519 --> 17:34.704
do you mind if I
use your restroom?

17:34.738 --> 17:36.071
It was a long
drive up here.

17:36.105 --> 17:38.590
No, go right ahead.

17:38.624 --> 17:40.089
We should do something
without him sometime.

17:40.124 --> 17:41.792
Do you like tapas?

17:41.826 --> 17:43.627
Someone invited me
out for that once.

17:43.662 --> 17:45.277
I thought they said
topless, so I was like,

17:45.311 --> 17:46.411
(high-pitch): what?

17:46.446 --> 17:47.447
That's a good story.

17:47.481 --> 17:48.847
Hey, you know,
that was really cool

17:48.881 --> 17:50.915
how you told us what
"Snowbird" was about.

17:50.949 --> 17:52.183
Well, you know,
that's just the way

17:52.217 --> 17:53.767
I always interpreted the song.

17:53.801 --> 17:55.100
But I suppose Gene probably

17:55.135 --> 17:56.770
could have explained
it better than I.

17:56.804 --> 17:58.436
Who's Gene?

17:58.470 --> 18:00.370
Gene MacLellan.
He wrote the song.

18:00.405 --> 18:02.474
I see.

18:02.509 --> 18:04.441
But you wrote all
your other songs, right?

18:04.475 --> 18:06.709
No, all of my songs were
written by other people.

18:06.743 --> 18:08.343
As a singer,
I've been blessed

18:08.377 --> 18:10.711
to interpret the works of
many gifted, sensitive

18:10.745 --> 18:11.980
songwriters.

18:12.014 --> 18:14.616
Oh.

18:14.650 --> 18:17.184
So, you're just
a big (bleep) liar?

18:17.218 --> 18:18.451
Sit back down.

18:18.469 --> 18:20.719
All right, come on, Stewie,
let's go home...

18:20.753 --> 18:22.305
(muffled grunts)

18:22.339 --> 18:23.821
Stewie, what the
hell are you doing?

18:23.855 --> 18:25.090
She's a fraud, Brian.
A fraud!

18:25.124 --> 18:26.774
She didn't write any
of her songs.

18:26.808 --> 18:28.708
Well, so, that's the case
with a lot of singers.

18:28.743 --> 18:30.709
It's a very common
industry practice.

18:30.744 --> 18:32.846
Well, then you're all a bunch of
frauds, aren't you?

18:32.878 --> 18:34.780
A bunch of frauds and
charlatans.

18:34.814 --> 18:36.279
You want to be a puppet?

18:36.314 --> 18:38.147
Then sing for your life,
puppet. Come on.

18:38.181 --> 18:41.751
♪ Spread your tiny wings
and fly away! ♪

18:41.785 --> 18:46.186
(muffled singing)

18:46.221 --> 18:47.956
Come on, Anne, sing along.

18:47.988 --> 18:50.324
You know it. You didn't write
it, but you know it, bitch.

18:50.358 --> 18:54.477
STEWIE AND ANNE:
♪ The one I love forever is untrue ♪

18:54.495 --> 18:59.281
♪ And if I could you know that
I would fly away with you. ♪

19:01.999 --> 19:04.250
Hey, Meg, you here for the
expired hot dogs?

19:04.285 --> 19:06.318
No, Carl, I'm
looking for Chris.

19:06.352 --> 19:08.285
I've looked everywhere
and I can't find him.

19:08.319 --> 19:10.787
Uh, I haven't seen him but
I'll keep an eye out.

19:10.822 --> 19:12.090
(knocking on glass)

19:12.124 --> 19:14.690
Hey, bozos, what do
you think of this?

19:14.725 --> 19:17.059
(laughing):
I did, I did it!

19:17.094 --> 19:18.326
You're crazy man.

19:18.360 --> 19:19.928
You're so crazy.

19:23.082 --> 19:24.598
(gunfire, men shouting on TV)

19:24.632 --> 19:26.732
I thought I told you
to clean up in here?

19:26.766 --> 19:28.268
And why aren't
you dressed?

19:28.301 --> 19:29.535
We're supposed
to have dinner

19:29.570 --> 19:31.136
with Judge
Maynard and Timmy.

19:31.170 --> 19:33.271
Yeah, okay, as soon
as I finish this level.

19:33.306 --> 19:34.672
No, get your coat on.

19:34.706 --> 19:37.041
It's a three-hour
drive to that cabin.

19:37.075 --> 19:38.807
Can't you just
cook something here?

19:38.842 --> 19:39.976
Excuse me?

19:40.010 --> 19:41.777
I am not going
to cook you dinner

19:41.811 --> 19:43.578
while you sit here
in your own mess

19:43.612 --> 19:45.079
playing your TV games.

19:45.114 --> 19:47.414
Oh-ho,
and the martyr returns.

19:47.448 --> 19:52.417
Chris, I... I think
it's time for you to go.

19:52.451 --> 19:53.817
Okay, I'll go.

19:53.852 --> 19:55.186
But I want you to know,

19:55.221 --> 19:57.254
I faked all
my light-headedness.

19:57.288 --> 19:58.754
Mm, good Kool-Aid.

19:58.788 --> 20:01.356
Whoa, whoa.

20:01.390 --> 20:02.692
Sound familiar?

20:05.846 --> 20:07.095
Oh, my God, Chris.

20:07.130 --> 20:08.297
Oh, there you are.

20:08.332 --> 20:09.864
I've been looking all
over for you.

20:09.898 --> 20:12.599
Why? You want to humiliate
me with more slave work?

20:12.633 --> 20:16.367
No, I...
I wanted to apologize.

20:16.401 --> 20:17.970
I should've never
blackmailed you.

20:18.004 --> 20:19.604
I'm really sorry.

20:19.638 --> 20:22.038
And I want you
to come home.

20:22.073 --> 20:23.905
I thought you said no
one would miss me?

20:23.939 --> 20:26.474
I said a lot of stupid
things I didn't mean, Chris.

20:26.509 --> 20:28.394
But I miss you.

20:28.428 --> 20:30.962
Thanks, Meg.
I miss you, too.

20:30.996 --> 20:32.896
You know,
living in that family,

20:32.931 --> 20:34.297
we both have
pretty crappy lives.

20:34.331 --> 20:35.798
And if we're
going to survive it,

20:35.833 --> 20:37.299
we can't turn on each other.

20:37.333 --> 20:38.866
We're all we have.

20:38.900 --> 20:40.101
I know.

20:40.136 --> 20:42.102
I'll never do anything
like that again.

20:42.137 --> 20:43.903
Do you think you can
ever forgive me?

20:43.938 --> 20:46.041
Yeah, I forgive you.

20:46.075 --> 20:47.941
(engine starting)

20:50.126 --> 20:52.093
(tires squealing)

20:58.132 --> 21:00.166
(crying)

21:00.200 --> 21:01.632
We broke up.

21:01.633 --> 22:02.633
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru